--- IR --->
// the one thing a declaration's position still decides: a class constructor's `$this` carries the heap
// allocation in its *initializer*, so the slot may be seated early but the malloc may not move - and the
// field write has to follow it rather than store through a handle nothing allocated yet
CHECK: define ptr @_Counter
CHECK: %this = alloca ptr
CHECK-NOT: %value_ptr = getelementptr
CHECK: call ptr @malloc
CHECK: store ptr %obj, ptr %this
CHECK: %value_ptr = getelementptr
// both locals are seated before either initializer stores, which is the hoist. emitted at the declaration
// each was written at, `store i32 0, ptr %i` would sit between the two allocas
CHECK: define i32 @_sum_pairs
CHECK: %i = alloca i32
CHECK: %acc = alloca i32
CHECK: store i32 0, ptr %i
CHECK: store i32 0, ptr %acc
// the loop body is a scope of its own and seats its local at *its* entry, inside the loop - so the slot a
// `Pair` needs zeroed is zeroed once per turn rather than once per call
CHECK: %p = alloca %Pair
// a declaration the flow never reaches is seated too. before the hoist it was never visited at all
CHECK: define i32 @_unreachable_decl
CHECK: %never = alloca %Pair
